x87 at 64-bit precision: multiply and fst m32 inline, no helper call.

A profile of 3DMark2001 SE's Lobby on patch 48's mode 3 had its x87
arithmetic helpers at ~15 % of the vCPU and the three pages of x87 code
that call them as the hottest generated code, where each call brings its
argument setup, the i128 result's unpacking and the spills of
call-clobbered registers. Two of the calls are short enough as TCG ops:

- fmul: the mantissas' 128-bit product (mulu2_i64: mul + umulh on
  aarch64, mul on x86-64), normalized by its top bit (it lies in
  [2^126, 2^128)), and x87s_pack_x80 -- x87f_pack_x as TCG ops: the
  pre-rounding exponent checked to 1..0x7ffd (else the slow block),
  nearest-even on the 64 bits below, a carry out of 2^64 into the
  exponent, PE unless sticky, a zero operand giving a signed zero.
- fst m32: the top 24 mantissa bits rounded nearest-even by the 40 below,
  the float's exponent range checked once after the rounding's carry
  (pre-round out of range and overflow both leave [0, 253]), a zero kept
  with its sign.

Both branch-free apart from the branch to the slow block, like the rest
of the fast path. + - / and fst m64 / fist stay helper calls: add and
subtract would need both paths computed and one selected to stay
label-free, about a hundred ops, no clear win over the call.

